## Functions That Answer Questions

Boolean functions return `true` or `false` — they're like a yes/no checkpoint. Name them like questions: `is_adult`, `can_vote`, `is_warm`.

### Age Checks

```xiom
fn is_adult(age: Int) -> Bool {
  return age >= 18;
}

fn can_vote(age: Int) -> Bool {
  return age >= 18;
}

fn is_senior(age: Int) -> Bool {
  return age >= 65;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(is_adult(15));   // false
  io.println(is_adult(25));   // true
  io.println(is_senior(70));  // true
  return 0;
}
```

### Temperature Checks

```xiom
fn is_freezing(temp: Int) -> Bool {
  return temp <= 0;
}

fn is_warm(temp: Int) -> Bool {
  return temp >= 25;
}

fn is_comfortable(temp: Int) -> Bool {
  return temp >= 18 && temp <= 25;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(is_freezing(-5));     // true
  io.println(is_comfortable(22));  // true
  return 0;
}
```

### Using Boolean Functions in if

```xiom
fn check_entry(age: Int, has_id: Bool) -> Str {
  if !is_adult(age) {
    return "Too young!";
  }
  if !has_id {
    return "Need ID!";
  }
  return "Welcome in!";
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(check_entry(20, true));   // Welcome in!
  io.println(check_entry(16, false));  // Too young!
  return 0;
}
```

Boolean functions make your `if` conditions readable: `if is_adult(age)` reads like English.

